# A constant index under try into an array whose length is known while compiling is E16's compile-time error, even
# though try asked for the bounds check (E16d) - and in a function generic over the length, per instantiation, so a
# generic cannot ask "element 2 if there is one" with try (only with G26's "if N > 2").
#     expected: compiles; third(x) is 6, third(y) is -1 (OUT_OF_BOUNDS taken by the default), try z[5] gives 9
#     actual:   error[E16] "index 2 is outside the array's 2 elements" (instantiated with N = 2), and at z[5]
# Found by the generator (a checked index into a fixed-length array, or a generic's Array<T, N> parameter, with a
# literal index). Cause: semantic.c's constant-index check for Array<T, N> does not look at whether the index is under
# try. A question for the rule, not only the code: E16 says a known-out-of-range index is an error, E16d that try is
# the opt-in check - under try the failure is defined, so the error rejects a well-defined program. The generator now
# writes a non-literal index there.
fn third(a Array<I32, <N>>&) I32 { return try a[2] catch default -1 }
fn main() ? {
    x Array<I32, 3> = I32[4, 5, 6]
    y Array<I32, 2> = I32[4, 5]
    assert third(x) == 6
    assert third(y) == -1
    z Array<I32, 2> = I32[1, 2]
    assert (try z[5] catch default 9) == 9
}
